<!--
  navigate_to_pose, with only the recoveries a BIPED can safely take.

  Identical to nav2_bt_navigator's
  navigate_to_pose_w_replanning_and_recovery.xml in its planning and control
  half — 1 Hz replanning, ClearEntireCostmap on a planner or controller failure,
  six top-level retries. The ONLY change is the RecoveryActions round-robin:
  upstream cycles Clear -> Spin -> Wait -> BackUp; we cycle Clear -> Wait.

  WHY, AND WHY THIS FILE HAS TO EXIST AT ALL.

  behavior_server runs `behavior_plugins: ["wait"]` deliberately: BackUp, Spin
  and DriveOnHeading are written for a wheeled base, and an autonomous
  spin-in-place on a biped, triggered by a costmap glitch, is a fall.

  But disabling the behaviours is only half the job. The default BT still
  REFERENCES <Spin> and <BackUp>, and bt_navigator resolves those action
  servers at load time, not at first use:

      "spin" action server not available after waiting for 1.00s
      Exception when loading BT: Action server spin not available
      Failed to bring up all requested nodes. Aborting bringup.

  So a behaviour we deliberately do not run stopped the entire bringup — and
  because bt_navigator sits before velocity_smoother in the lifecycle manager's
  node_names, it also took down the only path to /c3po/cmd_vel. Trimming the
  behaviours without pinning a matching tree is the same shape of mistake as
  trimming plugin_lib_names was: the config said one thing and the XML another.

  WHAT THE ROBOT DOES WHEN IT IS STUCK, NOW. It clears both costmaps, waits 5 s,
  and tries again, six times. If the obstacle was a stale costmap artefact the
  clear fixes it; if it was real, the robot waits rather than blindly rotating
  next to it. When it genuinely cannot proceed it fails the goal and says so,
  which for a 1.3 m humanoid standing next to a person is the correct outcome —
  an honest refusal beats an unsupervised recovery manoeuvre.

  RE-ADDING Spin OR BackUp MEANS THREE EDITS, NOT ONE: the plugin here, the
  behavior_plugins list in nav2_params.yaml, and a supervised window to watch
  the first one fire. Do them together or not at all.
-->
<root main_tree_to_execute="MainTree">
  <BehaviorTree ID="MainTree">
    <RecoveryNode number_of_retries="6" name="NavigateRecovery">
      <PipelineSequence name="NavigateWithReplanning">
        <RateController hz="1.0">
          <RecoveryNode number_of_retries="1" name="ComputePathToPose">
            <ComputePathToPose goal="{goal}" path="{path}" planner_id="GridBased"/>
            <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearGlobalCostmap-Context" service_name="global_costmap/clear_entirely_global_costmap"/>
          </RecoveryNode>
        </RateController>
        <RecoveryNode number_of_retries="1" name="FollowPath">
          <FollowPath path="{path}" controller_id="FollowPath"/>
          <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearLocalCostmap-Context" service_name="local_costmap/clear_entirely_local_costmap"/>
        </RecoveryNode>
      </PipelineSequence>
      <ReactiveFallback name="RecoveryFallback">
        <GoalUpdated/>
        <RoundRobin name="RecoveryActions">
          <Sequence name="ClearingActions">
            <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearLocalCostmap-Subtree" service_name="local_costmap/clear_entirely_local_costmap"/>
            <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearGlobalCostmap-Subtree" service_name="global_costmap/clear_entirely_global_costmap"/>
          </Sequence>
          <Wait wait_duration="5"/>
        </RoundRobin>
      </ReactiveFallback>
    </RecoveryNode>
  </BehaviorTree>
</root>
